task: consumer-claim
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/consumer-claim
worktree: ../okay-wt-mpmc

why: the operator's design (2026-09-07): a queue per producer, and
  consumers that take from them ATOMICALLY and ASYNCHRONOUSLY, so
  that one consumer processing what it took does not hold the others
  up. Half of it exists — `AdaptiveFifo` already gives each producer
  its own part — and the measured half that does not is exactly the
  consumer side: adding consumers costs both designs about 2.3x
  (four producers, elementwise: ring 904 -> 2 307, adaptive 510 ->
  1 039), because every consumer walks the same scan cursor and
  competes for the same part's head, one element at a time.

what (staged, measure between stages):
  1. EXCLUSIVE PART CLAIM for a drain: a consumer CASes a per-part
     claim, drains up to `max` into its own chunk, releases the claim,
     and only then processes. Two consumers never touch one part's
     head at the same time, and no claim is held while anything is
     processed. The scan starts at a per-consumer rotation so they do
     not convoy onto part 0.
  2. IF STAGE 1 IS NOT ENOUGH: a per-consumer STASH — one claimed
     drain refills a private batch that later elementwise receives are
     served from without touching shared state. Staged second because
     a stash is a place elements can die: the strong contract says an
     accepted element is delivered, so a stash needs a registry the
     channel drains on close, and that is a bigger change than the
     claim.

laws that must not move: every element delivered exactly once across
  P x C (TestManyToMany), the end seen by every consumer, drain on
  close, and the P x C deadlock law at capacity 4.

predict: stage 1 takes the four-producer four-consumer row from 1 039
  toward 600 (the one-consumer number is 510, and a claim should let
  two consumers work in parallel rather than in turns), with the
  one-consumer rows unmoved.
  Disqualifying: elementwise stays within 10% of today, which would
  mean the cost is the per-element hand-off through the channel's
  waiter machinery rather than the buffer's head, and then stage 2
  (the stash) is the only thing that can help.
